<h2>A 2004 crime, a 2012 conviction, a 2026 appeal</h2>
<p>The gap between offence and appellate judgment &mdash; twenty-two years &mdash; is itself part of this case&rsquo;s significance. The trial court (Additional Sessions Judge, Fast Track, 1st Court, Alipore) convicted three men in 2012 for the murder: Md. Nausad, Samser Ali and Sk. Abdul Hamid, in appeals C.R.A. 233 of 2012, 245 of 2012 and 238 of 2013 respectively, heard together. Over such a span, appellate courts test whether the evidentiary pillars &mdash; identification, recoveries, forensics &mdash; were built well enough to stand without the scaffolding of freshness.</p>

<h2>The four pillars the Bench tested</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Opportunity to observe.</strong> The driver (PW-3) was not a fleeting bystander. The assailants boarded the car he was driving and stayed with him through the episode, under halogen lighting and at intimate distance. Quality of observation is the foundation of identification evidence, and here it was exceptional.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The TI parade.</strong> Held in accordance with law, with the omissions alleged by the defence found minor. A parade is corroborative machinery; its purpose is to test memory before the court identification, and it did.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Recoveries and ballistics.</strong> Articles recovered pursuant to the accused&rsquo;s statements under Section 27 of the Evidence Act survived challenge; discrepancies in the recovery evidence were held minor, and the ballistic opinion tied the pistol to the crime.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The Section 313 examination.</strong> The court confirmed the incriminating circumstances were adequately put to the accused — the procedural safeguard that so often unsettles convictions when skipped was satisfied.</div>
  </div>

<h2>Section 34 without an overt act</h2>
<p>The defence pressed the familiar argument that specific roles were not brought home to each accused. The Bench&rsquo;s response restates settled law: where common intention is established, Section 34 IPC fastens liability on each participant without requiring proof of an overt act by every one of them. On this record, the court in fact found the individual roles established too &mdash; boarding at knifepoint, the assault, the shooting, the throat-slitting &mdash; making the appeals doubly difficult.</p>

<h2>The outcome</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Appeal</th><th>Result</th></tr>
  <tr><td>C.R.A. 233 of 2012 (Md. Nausad)</td><td>Dismissed; conviction affirmed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>C.R.A. 245 of 2012 (Samser Ali)</td><td>Dismissed; conviction affirmed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>C.R.A. 238 of 2013 (Sk. Abdul Hamid)</td><td>Dismissed; conviction affirmed</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The record now travels back to the trial court for compliance. For appellate practitioners, the case is a reminder that a single-witness conviction is not a weak conviction when the witness had time, light and proximity &mdash; and that attacking parade formalities without disturbing the substance of the observation rarely moves a Division Bench.</p>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the Calcutta High Court dated 25 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice.</div>
